  "authors": "Shahrazad M A Malek, Ivan Saika-Voivod, Styliani Consta",
  "field_subfield": "Physical Chemistry, Nanoscience",
  "type_of_paper": "Computational Study",
  "executive_summary": "This study investigates the spatial distribution of sodium ions in supercooled aqueous nanodroplets using molecular dynamics simulations and an analytical reference model. The authors demonstrate that at room temperature, the radial distribution of a single Na+ ion is nearly uniform within the droplet core, consistent with a newly proposed \"electrostatic confinement\" model that predicts a harmonic potential centered at the droplet's center of mass. However, at supercooled temperatures (200 K), the internal structure of the water changes significantly. A low-density core with tetrahedral ordering forms, surrounded by a high-density, more disordered subsurface. The simulations reveal that Na+ ions are expelled from this low-density tetrahedral core and preferentially reside in the denser subsurface region. This behavior contrasts with room temperature conditions and cannot be explained by the standard non-linear Poisson-Boltzmann equation, which assumes a homogeneous dielectric. The study highlights the critical role of solvent structure in determining ion location, with implications for atmospheric aerosols and mass spectrometry techniques like electrospray ionization. The findings suggest that the heterogeneous environment of supercooled droplets fundamentally alters ion solvation, potentially affecting chemical reactivity and the preservation of macromolecular conformations. The authors propose that this work serves as a starting point for understanding the structure of supercooled electrosprayed droplets used in native mass spectrometry.",
